<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip Louis II, Count of Hanau-Münzenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1576">1576</a>–<a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip Louis II (1576–1612) was Count of Hanau-Münzenberg from 1580. A Calvinist, in 1597 he invited Reformed refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a> to found the New Town of Hanau beside the old town, bringing gold-working, jewellery and textile trades that shaped the city's economy for centuries. He married Catharina <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Belgica</a> of Nassau, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-louis-ii-count-of-hanau-munzenberg.jpg" alt="Philip Louis II, Count of Hanau-Münzenberg" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dominicus Custos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philipp_ludwig_ii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
